// Visibility exposure across the interaction layer (typl §3.3, ridl §14.5,
// §16.1, §16.4): TYPL-005 from a public `interface`, RIDL-143 from a public
// `service`.
//
// Both escaped the checker entirely until issue #161. TYPL-005 was a step of
// the typl definition lowering, so the two declaration kinds E2 added —
// `interface` and `service` — were never checked at all, and a program that
// crosses the visibility boundary compiled with zero diagnostics. The payload
// half is the sharper one: the Rust backend then emits a `pub` trait over a
// `pub(crate)` type, which rustc's `private_interfaces` lint turns into a hard
// build failure for any consumer building with `-D warnings`.
//
// `internal interface Backdoor` below is the control. It names `internal`
// declarations in every position `Exposed` does and draws nothing, because the
// rule is about a *public* item naming a package-private one — rejecting it
// would be the over-rejection failure, not the fix.

package diag.ridl

internal type Handle : integer [0..255]
internal const HANDLE_MAX = 7
internal const HANDLE_SLOTS = 4

/// The control: package-private in full. An `internal` declaration may name
/// `internal` declarations freely — both sides generate package-private code
/// (ADR-0008 decision 7), so nothing leaks. It names an `internal` constant
/// from a clause and an `internal` bound from an array, so the two positions
/// found late are controlled here as well as provoked below.
internal interface Backdoor {
  signal handle : Handle @10ms
  signal HANDLE_MAX : Level @10ms
  fixed slots : [Level; 1..HANDLE_SLOTS]
  command trim(level: Level) [
    require level < HANDLE_MAX
  ]
  query drain(): Level [
    ensure result < HANDLE_MAX
  ]
}

/// A public interface over a package-private payload, parameter, return type,
/// collection length bound and contract constant — one TYPL-005 per exposure
/// family. The contract ones are included because IR v2 carries a clause as its
/// canonical source text (ADR-0008 decision 14) and both backends emit that text
/// as data, so a name in it that an importer cannot resolve leaks exactly as a
/// payload type does.
///
/// The signal `HANDLE_MAX` deliberately collides with the constant of the same
/// name, because the two clause kinds do not bind the same names: a `require`
/// sees the interface's own signals (ridl §13) and an `ensure` does not.
/// `trim`'s `require` reads the *signal* and exposes nothing; `drain`'s
/// `ensure` reads the *constant*, and does. A checker that treats the two
/// scopes alike is silently wrong in one direction or the other, and only a
/// pair like this tells them apart — the first version of this fix re-derived
/// the binding order from the syntax and accepted the `ensure` half in silence.
interface Exposed {
  signal handle : Handle @10ms
  signal HANDLE_MAX : Level @10ms
  fixed slots : [Level; 1..HANDLE_SLOTS]
  command bind(h: Handle)
  query readHandle(): Handle
  command trim(level: Level) [
    require level < HANDLE_MAX
  ]
  query drain(): Level [
    ensure result < HANDLE_MAX
  ]
}

/// A public service publishing a package-private shape (RIDL-143). This is the
/// one exposure TYPL-005's remedy cannot reach: a service takes no `internal`
/// modifier (ridl §14.5), so "mark the exposing declaration internal too" is
/// not an available fix, which is why it carries its own code.
service diag.showcase.backdoor : Backdoor
